associated with high titers of GAD-Ab,
which may respond clinically to γ-amino
butyric acid (GABA) agonists or intrave-
nous immunoglobulin. 2,3 Although the typ-
ical form of SPS predominantly affects the
lower extremities and lumbar paraspinal
muscles, variant forms are known to occur,
including a limited variant (stiff-limb
syndrome) and a form with progressive
encephalomyelitis, rigidity, and myoclo-
nus. In addition, some patients with a para-
neoplastic form of SPS, especially in
association with breast cancer, have anti-
bodies against amphiphysin, a presynaptic
cytosolic vesicle protein. 4,5
The presence of cervical and arm
involvement associated with a uniform ros-
trocaudal distribution was proposed to be
a hallmark of the paraneoplastic variant with
amphiphysin-Abs.1 This pattern was not pres-
ent in our patient, but a coexisting encepha-
lopathy akin to limbic encephalitis led to
suspicion of a paraneoplastic process and
the detection of occult breast cancer and am-
phiphysin-Abs. Treatment of the cancer
resulted in marked clinical improvement in
our patient. <seg id="s0004" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">Our patient 's presentation sup-
ports the recent characterization of amphi-
physin-Ab–associated paraneoplastic SPS in
females, nearly uniformly associated with
breast cancer, who dramatically respond to
treatment of the cancer. However, our case
highlights the need to be vigilant for paraneo-
plastic forms of SPS even when patients pres-
ent with otherwise typical SPS. The clinical
investigations should also be guided by the
rare possibilities of cancer in organs other
than the breast in patients with SPS with am-
phiphysin-Abs.6</seg>
